Environment variables in Next.js are loaded from `.env` files by default. These variables are typically accessed using a process.env variable name.
For example, if you have an `env` file with the following contents:
```
APP_NAME=My App
PORT=3000

DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:port/%s

JWT_SECRET_KEY=supersecretkey

# Other environment variables and their respective meanings
APP_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE=environment_variable_name
APP_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE_ROOT_PATHPATH=/path/to/environment_variable_name/root_pathpath
```
And your Next.js app might look something like this:
```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { BrowserRouter as Router, Route } from 'react-router-dom';

function App() {
  return (
    <Router>
      <Route path="/env" component={Env}} />
      <Route path="/" component={Home}} />
    </Router>
  );
}

function Env() {
  const envVariable = process.env['APP_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE'];
  if (envVariable) {
    return (
      <ul>
        <li>{ envVariable }}</li>
      </ul>
    );
  } else {
    return (
      <ul>
        <li>Environment variable not set</li>
      </ul>
    );
  }
}

function Home() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Welcome to My App</h1>
      <p>This is the home page of an application named My App. The app uses Next.js as its frontend framework.</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this example, Next.js is configured to load environment variables from a `.env` file by default. The environment variables are accessible using the `process.env` object.
The `App` component serves as the entry point of the Next.js app. It defines the overall structure and behavior of the app.
On the other hand, the `Home` component represents the home page of the Next.js app. It contains a div element that houses the app's main content.
In this example, the `Home` component is rendered as the first content on the root level of the Next.js app.
The rendered `Home` component provides a simple structure for the home page of an application called My App.
